@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security;architecture] personal-auth-never-adopts-operator-authority
Personal authorization MUST use only the fixed Pully-owned OAuth configuration and
the verified user's GitHub credential. Guest, missing, revoked, expired or failed
personal authorization MUST NOT fall back to environment/CLI GitHub credentials,
legacy account data or operator caches. This additive module MUST NOT activate a
public listener or expose legacy RPC. Source: Tavi's 21 Oct 2026 any-account GitHub
sign-in and anonymous public-browsing decision and its approved multiuser plan.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security] personal-vault-authenticates-its-stable-owner
GitHub access and refresh tokens MUST remain server-side and encrypted at rest with
authenticated encryption under an operator-supplied versioned key. The authenticated
context MUST include the stable account ID, immutable GitHub subject, credential
revision, provider and record format/key version. An unknown key, modified record or
record copied between accounts/revisions MUST fail without returning credentials.
No token, key or plaintext payload MAY appear in Debug output, status, logs or errors.
Source: Tavi's 21 Oct 2026 encrypted per-user credential-vault and identity plan.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security] personal-oauth-fixed-client-and-bounded-exchange
Authorization and token exchange MUST use the operator-configured Pully OAuth App,
fixed GitHub.com endpoints, a fixed HTTPS callback, random state and S256 PKCE.
Native input MUST NOT replace the app credentials, callback, provider or token URL.
Exchange MUST refuse redirects and bound its time, response and credential sizes;
provider error text MUST NOT escape. Every acquired access token MUST be verified
against GitHub's authenticated user before use. Source: Tavi's 21 Oct 2026 personal
GitHub authentication plan and GitHub's current OAuth App authorization docs.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security;product] personal-app-authority-is-not-inherited-scope
Public-read sign-in MUST retain public-read application authority even if GitHub
returns previously granted broader scopes. Private repository authority MUST require
an explicit upgrade and its actual repo grant. Expiring access/refresh responses
MUST retain both expirations and rotate the pair; reduced grants MUST NOT preserve
broader application authority. Scope MUST NOT bypass organization approval or SSO.
Source: Tavi's 21 Oct 2026 public-first and explicit private-access decision and the
current GitHub OAuth App scope and refresh documentation.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security;architecture] personal-session-is-bound-to-one-stable-user
Persisted personal sessions MUST contain only hashes of high-entropy Pully session
bearers. Session claims MUST authenticate their own hash, stable account, immutable
GitHub subject, generation and expiry, independently of credential envelopes. A
copied or expired session MUST NOT return a credential. Sign-out MUST revoke the
matching captured authority; unlink MUST revoke every captured authority and session
for that account. After an awaited identity verification, the current session and
credential revision MUST be rechecked before authority is published. Source: Tavi's
21 Oct 2026 per-user vault, revocation and cross-user isolation decision.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security] personal-legacy-link-requires-two-current-authorities
A legacy account link MUST require the actual authenticated legacy session and a
verified GitHub authorization. The legacy session MUST be rechecked when installing
the link. Email/login matching MUST NOT link identities, and an already-linked
immutable GitHub subject MUST NOT be reassigned or silently merge accounts. Linking
MUST NOT adopt legacy tokens, caches, account keys or unrelated data. Source: Tavi's
21 Oct 2026 compatibility and explicit linking decision.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security;architecture] personal-auth-native-proof-stays-in-bodies
The fixed personal-auth wire protocol MUST keep the native attempt proof and Pully
session out of browser URLs, callback URLs, logs and errors. A native client names
only its own random proof challenge; it MUST NOT supply provider/app configuration,
redirects, target accounts or application authority to public sign-in. A successful
single-use claim MAY return the newly issued Pully session once in its HTTPS body.
Unix timestamp fields MUST use seconds and pollAfterMs MUST use milliseconds.
Source: Tavi's 21 Oct 2026 server-owned authorization and bounded native handoff plan.
